From: mscharged-port contributors
Subject: [PATCH] Parse the GX FIFO inline on Apple Silicon
Upstream-base: d664382f57002fbff143911c81b06b7e7a4b3ab4
Classification: platform workaround. Apply after 0081.

With the FIFO worker thread, lit 3D renders about half as bright on Apple
Silicon Macs during live play; the same build is correct when the FIFO is
parsed on the GX owner thread (verified on the owner's Mac at 59.9 FPS in a
match). A worker delayed by 3 ms per batch does not reproduce it on x86, so
the cause is an unresolved cross-thread ordering issue rather than lag.
Until it is found, Apple Silicon uses the existing inline mode by default;
AURORA_DEBUG_FIFO_INLINE=0/1 still forces either mode.

diff --git a/lib/gx/fifo.cpp b/lib/gx/fifo.cpp
index 19371c5..bfd1c40 100644
--- a/lib/gx/fifo.cpp
+++ b/lib/gx/fifo.cpp
@@ -133,6 +133,13 @@ void worker_main(std::stop_token token) noexcept {
     const uint64_t processed = sProcessed.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
     const uint64_t published = sPublished.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
     if (published != processed) {
+      // AURORA_DEBUG_FIFO_DELAY_US=N lets the worker fall N microseconds behind
+      // per batch, to reproduce worker races on fast platforms (diagnosis only).
+      static const long delayUs = [] {
+        const char* value = std::getenv("AURORA_DEBUG_FIFO_DELAY_US");
+        return value ? std::strtol(value, nullptr, 10) : 0L;
+      }();
+      if (delayUs > 0) std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::microseconds(delayUs));
       process_to(published, std::memory_order_release);
       continue;
     }
